{
  "question": "Does Westpac's low FY25 impairment charge show that credit-risk normalisation is complete, or does the September 2025 provision stock still embed material caution? Reconcile the charge flow, the CAP/IAP movements, overlays and the probability-weighted/base/downside ECL scenarios.",
  "bank": "WBC",
  "periods": [
    "FY25"
  ],
  "answer": "The low FY25 impairment charge (4.0 bps avg loans) [ev-19] does not indicate complete credit-risk normalisation; the Sept 2025 provision stock ($4,978m) [ev-1] embeds material caution. While modelled CAPs fell $168m due to improved property/interest rate outlooks and lower delinquencies [ev-5], overlays rose $59m to $238m [ev-3] for portfolio seasoning and geographic stress. Crucially, the downside scenario weight increased by 5ppt to 47.5% [ev-10], reflecting geopolitical instability. Total provisions remain $1.9bn above base case ECL [ev-41]. Thus, the low charge reflects strong model inputs offsetting heightened scenario weighting and proactive overlays, signaling continued prudence rather than normalized risk.",
  "key_facts": [
    {
      "fact": "Total provisions decreased 2% to $4,987m, driven by a $168m reduction in modelled CAPs.",
      "evidence": [
        "ev-5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "Overlays increased $59m to $238m due to portfolio seasoning and geographic stress not in models.",
      "evidence": [
        "ev-3",
        "ev-6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "Downside scenario weight increased 5ppt to 47.5%, reflecting geopolitical instability.",
      "evidence": [
        "ev-5",
        "ev-10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "Impairment charges were 4.0 bps of average loans, down from 7.0 bps in FY24.",
      "evidence": [
        "ev-19",
        "ev-28"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "Provisions are $1.9bn above base case ECL, indicating embedded caution.",
      "evidence": [
        "ev-41"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 90,
